const unit6={
id:6,title:'Lipids and cardiovascular health',headline:'Follow the fat.<br>From structure to circulation.',intro:'Learn fatty-acid structure, essential fats, triglycerides, digestion, lipoproteins and heart-health connections across 12 topics.',quizNote:'All 30 source questions are included. Feedback explains simplified or outdated wording and distinguishes course-key logic from current nuance.',
lessons:[
['Fatty-acid anatomy','Read the two ends and the carbon chain.','<p>A fatty acid is a hydrocarbon chain with a <b>carboxylic acid group</b> at the alpha end and a <b>methyl group</b> at the omega end. Chain length and the number, position and geometry of double bonds influence physical and metabolic properties.</p><h3>Alpha and omega are directions</h3><p>Alpha numbering begins at the carboxyl side. Omega numbering begins at the methyl side. An omega-3 fatty acid has its first double bond between the third and fourth carbons counted from the methyl end.</p><div class="example"><b>Read 18:2</b><p>The first number gives 18 carbons; the second gives 2 carbon-carbon double bonds. It is therefore polyunsaturated. The notation needs an n-3 or n-6 designation to reveal its omega family.</p></div><p>Longer chains generally have stronger intermolecular attractions and higher melting points than otherwise similar shorter chains. Cooking stability also depends strongly on unsaturation, refining and the whole oil.</p>'],
['Saturated, mono- and polyunsaturated','Count double bonds and predict shape.','<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Double bonds</th><th>Common pattern</th></tr><tr><td>Saturated</td><td>0</td><td>Straighter chains pack closely</td></tr><tr><td>Monounsaturated</td><td>1</td><td>One cis kink</td></tr><tr><td>Polyunsaturated</td><td>2 or more</td><td>Multiple kinks; more oxidation-sensitive</td></tr></table><p>A saturated fatty acid has only single carbon-carbon bonds. An unsaturated fatty acid has at least one double bond.</p><div class="example"><b>Compound butter</b><p>Butter contains many fatty acids but is relatively rich in saturated fatty acids and is commonly classified as a saturated-fat food. Herbs change flavour, not that broad classification.</p></div><p>Food fats are mixtures. Calling olive oil “unsaturated” or butter “saturated” identifies the predominant pattern, not every molecule.</p>'],
['Cis, trans and hydrogenation','See how geometry changes a fat.','<p>Most naturally occurring unsaturated fatty acids have <b>cis</b> double bonds. A cis bond bends the chain and reduces tight packing. A <b>trans</b> bond leaves a straighter shape that behaves more like a saturated chain.</p><p>Industrial partial hydrogenation adds hydrogen to some double bonds and can convert some cis bonds to trans bonds. Fully hydrogenated oil contains little or no trans fat but is more saturated.</p><div class="example"><b>Label reading</b><p>“0 g trans fat” can be affected by serving-size rounding. Partially hydrogenated oil in an ingredient list is a clue to industrial trans fat, depending on local rules and formulation.</p></div><p>Trans fats tend to raise LDL cholesterol and can lower HDL cholesterol. A practical pattern is to keep industrial trans fat as low as possible and replace saturated fats with unsaturated fats.</p>'],
['Omega families and essential fats','Locate the first double bond from the methyl end.','<p>Omega-3 and omega-6 describe the first double bond counted from the methyl end. Humans cannot place double bonds at certain omega positions, so <b>alpha-linolenic acid (ALA, omega-3)</b> and <b>linoleic acid (LA, omega-6)</b> are essential.</p><div class="example"><b>Food sources</b><p>Canola, soybean and flax oils, walnuts and chia provide ALA. Many vegetable oils, nuts and seeds provide LA. Fatty fish and seafood provide EPA and DHA.</p></div><p>Conversion of ALA to EPA and especially DHA is limited and variable. EPA and DHA are important, but ALA is the omega-3 formally considered essential for people over age one in the DRI framework.</p><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Omega3FattyAcids-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH: omega-3 fatty acids</a></p>'],
['Eicosanoids and life-stage context','Connect fatty acids with local signalling.','<p>Eicosanoids are short-lived signalling molecules made from 20-carbon PUFAs. Arachidonic acid is an omega-6 precursor; EPA is an omega-3 precursor. Their products participate in inflammation, vascular tone, platelet activity and immune regulation.</p><p>The quiz traces these pathways to the essential families: linoleic acid can contribute to arachidonic acid, while alpha-linolenic acid can contribute to EPA. “Omega-6 is inflammatory” is an oversimplification.</p><div class="example"><b>Infant nutrition nuance</b><p>DHA and arachidonic acid are important in developing tissues. Human milk supplies them in varying amounts, and modern formula composition varies by country. Breastfeeding recommendations rest on many nutritional, immunological and relational factors - not one lipid alone.</p></div>'],
['Triglycerides and energy storage','Build the body’s main storage lipid.','<p>A triglyceride contains one <b>glycerol</b> molecule bonded to three fatty acids. Triglycerides are the major form of fat in food and the major stored lipid in adipose tissue.</p><p>When energy intake exceeds immediate needs, excess energy from fat, carbohydrate or protein can contribute to triglyceride storage. Stored triglycerides can later release fatty acids for energy.</p><div class="example"><b>Adipocytes are active cells</b><p>Adipose tissue stores energy, cushions organs, insulates the body and produces signalling molecules. It is not a passive “fat depository.”</p></div><p>Very low subcutaneous fat in severe undernutrition can reduce insulation. <b>Lanugo</b> - fine, downy hair - can appear as an adaptation, although it is not specific to one condition.</p>'],
['Phospholipids, membranes and cholesterol','Separate three important lipid families.','<p><b>Phospholipids</b> are amphipathic: their polar head interacts with water while nonpolar tails avoid it. In water they form bilayers, creating the basic barrier of cell membranes.</p><p><b>Cholesterol</b> is a sterol used in membranes and as a precursor for bile acids, steroid hormones and vitamin D. Plant foods do not contain dietary cholesterol in the usual label sense; animal-derived foods do.</p><div class="example"><b>Why amphipathic matters</b><p>A phospholipid can face water with its head while shielding lipid with its tails. The same organizing principle helps lipids travel in lipoprotein particles.</p></div><p>Blood cholesterol is not simply cholesterol eaten. The liver synthesizes it, and blood levels reflect genetics, dietary pattern, metabolic health and lipoprotein handling.</p>'],
['Digesting and absorbing fat','Follow fat from a meal into circulation.','<ol><li>Mechanical mixing disperses food fat.</li><li>In the duodenum, bile emulsifies large droplets.</li><li>Cholecystokinin (CCK) stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic secretion.</li><li>Pancreatic lipase hydrolyses triglycerides mainly into monoglycerides and free fatty acids.</li><li>Mixed micelles deliver digestion products to enterocytes.</li><li>Long-chain lipids are reassembled and packaged into chylomicrons.</li><li>Chylomicrons enter lymph and then blood.</li></ol><div class="example"><b>Bile is not an enzyme</b><p>Bile emulsifies fat; lipases catalyse triglyceride hydrolysis. These are complementary but different jobs.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/digestive-system-how-it-works" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIDDK: how digestion works</a></p>'],
['Lipoprotein transport','Track chylomicrons, VLDL, LDL and HDL.','<p>Because many lipids are poorly soluble in water, they circulate in <b>lipoproteins</b>: particles with a hydrophobic core and an amphipathic surface of phospholipids, free cholesterol and proteins.</p><table><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Main teaching role</th></tr><tr><td>Chylomicron</td><td>Moves dietary triglyceride from intestinal cells</td></tr><tr><td>VLDL</td><td>Moves liver-made triglyceride toward tissues</td></tr><tr><td>LDL</td><td>Delivers cholesterol; excess exposure contributes to atherosclerosis</td></tr><tr><td>HDL</td><td>Participates in reverse cholesterol transport</td></tr></table><p><b>Lipoprotein lipase</b> on capillary surfaces hydrolyses triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL.</p><div class="example"><b>Do not swap the origins</b><p>Intestinal cells assemble chylomicrons from absorbed dietary lipids. The liver assembles VLDL from endogenous lipid.</p></div>'],
['Lipids and cardiovascular risk','Interpret biomarkers without reducing health to one number.','<p>Higher LDL particle exposure is causally related to atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. HDL cholesterol is associated with risk, but raising HDL-C by itself is not a universal treatment target.</p><p>Saturated and trans fats can raise LDL cholesterol; replacing them with unsaturated fats tends to improve the lipid profile. Soluble fibre can reduce LDL cholesterol. EPA and DHA can lower triglycerides, especially at therapeutic doses under clinical supervision.</p><div class="example"><b>A breakfast pattern</b><p>Oatmeal with berries and lower-fat milk combines soluble fibre with a relatively low saturated-fat profile. One food does not determine an outcome, but repeated patterns matter.</p></div><p>C-reactive protein is a nonspecific inflammation marker associated with cardiovascular risk. Assessment also considers blood pressure, diabetes, smoking, family history and other factors.</p>'],
['Intake ranges and practical choices','Turn percentages into food-pattern decisions.','<p><b>Total fat AMDR for adults:</b> 20-35% of energy. For 2400 kcal, that is 480-840 kcal from fat, or about 53-93 g using 9 kcal/g.</p><div class="example"><b>Saturated-fat ceiling</b><p>Ten percent of 2700 kcal is 270 kcal, or 30 g. “Less than 10%” means the target is below that ceiling; individual therapeutic targets may be lower.</p></div><p>Choose mostly unsaturated-fat sources such as nuts, seeds, avocado, fish and liquid non-tropical vegetable oils, while considering total energy and what they replace.</p><p>Alcohol is not a lipid treatment. It can raise triglycerides; although moderate intake may be associated with higher HDL-C, alcohol has other risks and people should not start for heart health.</p><p><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-macronutrients.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Health Canada: macronutrient reference values</a></p>'],
['Connect and review','Rehearse the complete lipid story.','<div class="example"><b>From olive oil to a muscle cell</b><p>Triglycerides enter the small intestine, are emulsified by bile and hydrolysed by lipase. Enterocytes absorb the products, rebuild triglycerides and package them into chylomicrons. In capillaries, lipoprotein lipase releases fatty acids for tissue use or storage.</p></div><h3>Explain it without looking</h3><ul><li>What is found at the alpha and omega ends?</li><li>How do saturated, monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids differ?</li><li>What does 18:2 mean?</li><li>Why are ALA and LA essential?</li><li>How do triglycerides, phospholipids and cholesterol differ?</li><li>What do bile, CCK and pancreatic lipase each do?</li><li>How do chylomicrons and VLDL differ?</li><li>Why is “HDL is good and LDL is bad” incomplete?</li><li>How do you calculate an AMDR range?</li></ul><p>The practice section contains all 30 source items. Explanations show the intended answer and add present-day nuance where wording is simplified.</p>']
],
questions:[
{n:1,q:'What does the alpha (α) end of a fatty acid contain?',o:['A phosphorus atom','A carboxylic acid group','A methyl group','A nitrogen atom'],a:1,e:'The alpha end is the carboxylic acid end. The opposite omega end terminates in a methyl group.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:2,q:'Nigel wants a stir-frying lipid that is more stable and will not smoke and burn as readily at medium-high heat. He should select a lipid that contains _____ fatty acids.',o:['mostly long-chain','mostly short-chain','a mix of short- and medium-chain','a mix of long- and medium-chain'],a:0,e:'Mostly long-chain is the intended answer because shorter chains are more volatile. In practice, smoke point and stability also depend on unsaturation, refining, free fatty acids, antioxidants and reuse.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:3,q:'A saturated fatty acid contains _____ carbon-to-carbon bonds.',o:['only single','only double','more single than double','more double than single'],a:0,e:'Saturated means the carbon chain contains no carbon-carbon double bonds.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:4,q:'A fatty acid containing two or more carbon-carbon double bonds in its backbone is a(n) _____ fatty acid.',o:['saturated','unsaturated','monounsaturated','polyunsaturated','a and c','b and d'],a:5,e:'It is both unsaturated and, more specifically, polyunsaturated. Therefore “b and d” is correct.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:5,q:'Susan makes compound butter by softening butter, mixing in seasoning and chilling it. What type of fat would it be classified as?',o:['Saturated','Unsaturated','Monounsaturated','Polyunsaturated','a and c','b and d'],a:0,e:'Butter is a mixture but is commonly classified as a saturated-fat food because saturated fatty acids predominate relative to most liquid oils.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:6,q:'Lea wants to limit trans fatty acids. Trans fats are _____.',o:['typically liquids at room temperature','generally found in foods containing PUFAs','produced commercially via partial hydrogenation','generally found in non-organic foods'],a:2,e:'Industrial trans fats are produced during partial hydrogenation. Small natural amounts can also occur in ruminant foods.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:7,q:'A fatty acid designated as 18:2:',o:['has 18 carbons.','has 2 double bonds.','is unsaturated.','is polyunsaturated.','a and b only','a, b, c, and d'],a:5,e:'18:2 means 18 carbons and 2 double bonds. It is both unsaturated and polyunsaturated, so all four statements are true.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:8,q:'An omega-3 fatty acid has its first double bond _____ end of the molecule.',o:['between the third and fourth carbons from the carboxylic acid','between the second and third carbons from the carboxylic acid','between the second and third carbons from the methyl','between the third and fourth carbons from the methyl'],a:3,e:'Omega numbering starts at the methyl end. Omega-3 means the first double bond begins at carbon 3.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:9,q:'From which fatty acids are the eicosanoids that assist and regulate the immune and cardiovascular systems made?',o:['Linoleic and arachidonic','Palmitic and arachidonic','Linolenic and palmitic','Linolenic and linoleic'],a:3,e:'The course-level answer traces eicosanoid families to linoleic and alpha-linolenic acids. More directly, many are made from longer-chain derivatives such as arachidonic acid and EPA.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:10,q:'Which reason supports promoting breastfeeding over formula feeding in the source lesson?',o:['Formula lacks some conditionally essential long-chain PUFAs','Formula provides too much long-chain PUFAs','Formula provides too much conditionally essential MUFAs','Formula lacks some conditionally essential MUFAs'],a:0,e:'A is the intended historical course answer. Human milk supplies DHA and arachidonic acid, but many current formulas contain added DHA and ARA; breastfeeding guidance rests on more than one lipid.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:11,q:'Which food selection is most likely to provide essential fatty acids?',o:['A salad with oil and vinegar dressing','A strawberry-banana smoothie','A chicken salad whole-wheat wrap','A fortified sports beverage'],a:0,e:'Vegetable oil is the clearest source of linoleic acid and, depending on the oil, alpha-linolenic acid.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:12,q:'Triglycerides consist of a _____ molecule bonded to three fatty acids.',o:['glycerol','glycogen','glucose','glucagon'],a:0,e:'A triglyceride has one glycerol backbone esterified to three fatty acids.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:13,q:'A triglyceride is composed of:',o:['glycerol.','three fatty acids.','omega-3 fatty acids only.','a and b','b and c','All of the above'],a:3,e:'A triglyceride contains glycerol plus three fatty acids. They need not be omega-3s.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:14,q:'Fatty acids not required for energy or other functions are stored as _____ in specialized cells called _____.',o:['triglycerides, adipocytes','fatty acids, fat depositories','glycerol, lipid silos','ketones, ketose depositories'],a:0,e:'Excess fatty acids are stored mainly as triglycerides within adipocytes.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:15,q:'Which development can indicate insufficient subcutaneous adipose tissue?',o:['Wrinkled skin','Lanugo','Muscle weakness','Lipiditis'],a:1,e:'Lanugo can appear in severe undernutrition as insulation is lost. It is a sign requiring broader assessment, not a diagnosis alone.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:16,q:'Cell membranes serve as barriers because they are made of _____ phospholipids.',o:['amphipathic','hydrophilic','hydrophobic','lipogenic'],a:0,e:'Phospholipids are amphipathic: hydrophilic heads face water and hydrophobic tails form the membrane interior.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:17,q:'Because lipids are not water soluble, they require _____ to facilitate circulation.',o:['phospholipids','sodium','bile acids','cholesterol esters'],a:0,e:'Phospholipids help form water-compatible lipoprotein surfaces. Apolipoproteins are also essential but are not an option.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:18,q:'Which food provides the most cholesterol in a 3-ounce serving?',o:['Peanut butter','Milk','Avocado','Bagel'],a:1,e:'Milk is animal-derived and contains cholesterol; the plant foods listed contain essentially none. Amount varies with milk fat level.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:19,q:'Which enzymes digest triglycerides?',o:['Lipidases','Fat depredators','Fat degradators','Lipases'],a:3,e:'Lipases hydrolyse triglycerides. Pancreatic lipase performs most digestion in the small intestine.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:20,q:'What hormone stimulates gallbladder contraction and bile release into the duodenum?',o:['Retinolase (RNS)','Cholecystokinin (CCK)','Insulin','Glucagon'],a:1,e:'CCK responds to fat and protein in the small intestine and stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic secretion.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:21,q:'What enzyme enables muscle and adipose tissue to release fatty acids from circulating chylomicrons?',o:['Lipoprotein dehydrogenase','Lipid synthetase','Lipid oxygenase','Lipoprotein lipase'],a:3,e:'Lipoprotein lipase at capillary surfaces hydrolyses triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:22,q:'Chylomicrons are made from ingested fat by the _____ while VLDL can be made from excess carbohydrate, lipid or protein by the _____.',o:['liver, adipocytes','gallbladder, adipocytes','intestinal cells, liver','stomach, pancreas'],a:2,e:'Intestinal cells assemble chylomicrons from dietary lipids. The liver assembles VLDL to export endogenous triglyceride.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:23,q:'Which dietary fats can increase LDL concentrations?',o:['PUFAs and alpha-9 fatty acids','omega-3 and cis fatty acids','fiber and omega-6 fatty acids','saturated and trans fatty acids'],a:3,e:'Saturated fat can raise LDL, and industrial trans fat has an adverse lipoprotein effect. Replacement nutrient and total pattern matter.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:24,q:'With a diet high in PUFAs, omega-3 fatty acids or dietary fibre, LDL levels will probably:',o:['decrease.','stay the same.','increase.','None of the above'],a:0,e:'Decrease is intended, especially when unsaturated fats replace saturated/trans fats and soluble fibre is included. Omega-3s more consistently lower triglycerides than LDL.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:25,q:'Under the AMDR, how much of a 2400-kcal intake should come from lipid?',o:['200-240 kcal','250-330 kcal','360-470 kcal','480-840 kcal'],a:3,e:'Adult fat AMDR is 20-35%: 2400 × 0.20 = 480 kcal and 2400 × 0.35 = 840 kcal.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:26,q:'HDLs deliver cholesterol to tissues that use it for structural and metabolic purposes.',o:['True','False'],a:1,e:'False. LDL is the main class associated with delivery to tissues. HDL participates in reverse cholesterol transport and lipid exchange.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:27,q:'A high concentration of which marker is related to increased cardiovascular disease risk?',o:['C-reactive protein','Albumin','Blood glucose','HDL cholesterol'],a:0,e:'High C-reactive protein is associated with inflammation and cardiovascular risk. It is nonspecific and needs clinical context.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:28,q:'Which frequent food is most likely to decrease blood cholesterol and triglyceride levels?',o:['Cheeseburger','Olive-oil salad dressing','Oatmeal with blueberries and 1% milk','Tuna salad sandwich'],a:2,e:'Oatmeal supplies beta-glucan soluble fibre, which can lower LDL. Exact triglyceride response depends on the total diet and metabolism.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:29,q:'Moderate alcohol consumption tends to increase _____, while any alcohol tends to increase _____.',o:['triglycerides, HDL cholesterol','LDL cholesterol, HDL cholesterol','HDL cholesterol, triglycerides','HDL cholesterol, LDL cholesterol'],a:2,e:'The intended pair is HDL cholesterol and triglycerides. Higher HDL does not make alcohol a treatment; alcohol has other health risks.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:30,q:'At a 10% saturated-fat limit, how many of 2700 kcal would be the upper boundary?',o:['270','340','405','485'],a:0,e:'2700 × 0.10 = 270 kcal. Guidance says less than 10%, so this is a boundary, not a target.',unscored:false,image:null}
],
cards:[['Alpha end','Carboxylic acid end.'],['Omega end','Methyl end used for omega numbering.'],['18:2','18 carbons and 2 double bonds; polyunsaturated.'],['Saturated fatty acid','No carbon-carbon double bonds.'],['MUFA','One carbon-carbon double bond.'],['PUFA','Two or more carbon-carbon double bonds.'],['Cis versus trans','Cis bends the chain; trans leaves it straighter.'],['Partial hydrogenation','A process that can create industrial trans fats.'],['Omega-3 position','First double bond between carbons 3 and 4 from the methyl end.'],['Essential fatty acids','Linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid.'],['Triglyceride','One glycerol plus three fatty acids.'],['Adipocyte','Stores triglyceride and has endocrine functions.'],['Amphipathic phospholipid','Water-interacting head and water-avoiding tails.'],['Bile','Emulsifies fat; not an enzyme.'],['CCK','Stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic secretion.'],['Lipase','Hydrolyses triglycerides.'],['Chylomicron','Carries dietary triglyceride from intestinal cells.'],['VLDL','Carries triglyceride assembled by the liver.'],['Lipoprotein lipase','Releases fatty acids from chylomicrons and VLDL.'],['LDL','Excess particle exposure promotes atherosclerosis.'],['HDL','Participates in reverse cholesterol transport.'],['Adult fat AMDR','20-35% of energy.'],['Saturated-fat math','Total kcal × 0.10 gives a 10% boundary.'],['Heart-health swap','Replace saturated and trans fats with unsaturated fats.']]
};
